## 11. SEO — IMPLEMENT AND VERIFY

SEO must be considered throughout the architecture, not added as an afterthought.

Technical SEO:

- Use semantic HTML5.
- Use a meaningful page title for every route.
- Provide unique meta descriptions.
- Configure canonical URLs appropriately for the deployment domain.
- Generate a valid XML sitemap.
- Configure robots.txt without accidentally blocking public pages or essential assets.
- Use clean, descriptive URLs.
- Add Open Graph and social-sharing metadata where appropriate.
- Use meaningful internal links.
- Use breadcrumb navigation and appropriate structured data.
- Ensure product, category, article, and brand content is present in the rendered page.
- Avoid duplicate titles and descriptions.
- Handle nonexistent routes with a proper 404 page.
- Avoid broken links and empty indexable pages.
- Ensure the mobile experience is usable and crawlable.

Structured data:

- Use valid JSON-LD for appropriate entities such as Organization, WebSite, BreadcrumbList, Product, and Article.
- Add Product offers, prices, availability, and review information only when the underlying data genuinely supports them.
- Never fabricate ratings, reviews, business addresses, shipping policies, or availability.
- Validate structured data before release.
- Do not add every schema type to every page.

For an Astro-based implementation, use appropriate prerendering or static generation for public product, category, and article pages where feasible. If React is used as the main application framework, use an appropriate prerendering or server-rendering strategy when supported by the environment.

Do not assume that a client-only page automatically provides the best SEO result.

SEO content must remain readable and useful to humans. Do not keyword-stuff titles, headings, alt text, or descriptions.

Do not promise a specific Google ranking or guarantee that all SEO scores will be 100%.

